<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CriKey per-user MSI, WiX v5 (WixToolset.Sdk / the `wix` dotnet tool).

  WHY v4 AND NOT v3
  =================
  v3 is in maintenance and needs candle.exe and light.exe from a machine-wide
  installer; v4 is a `dotnet tool` the build script can install into the
  repository and pin. The `Files` harvesting element used below for the two
  Python payload trees is also v4-only: under v3 the same job needs `heat.exe`
  to generate a fragment on every build, and a generated fragment that nobody
  reviews is exactly where an unwanted file gets shipped from.

  WHY PER-USER
  ============
  Scope="perUser" installs under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\CriKey, needs no
  elevation, and matches where the launcher's own data already lives
  (`StandardDirectories::windows` puts config in %APPDATA%\CriKey and cache in
  %LOCALAPPDATA%\CriKey). A launcher is a per-user tool: it registers a global
  hotkey for one desktop session, discovers that user's Start Menu, and runs
  that user's plugins. There is nothing in it a machine-wide install would
  share, and asking for an administrator prompt to get it buys the user
  nothing.

  WHAT VALIDATION SAYS ABOUT THAT, AND WHY IT IS STILL PER-USER
  =============================================================
  `wix build`, the .NET tool this repository drives, does not run ICE
  validation: only an MSBuild .wixproj validates automatically, and the .NET
  tool exposes validation as the separate `wix msi validate` subcommand
  (https://docs.firegiant.com/wix/tools/validation/). The build below
  therefore produces an .msi as written. `build.ps1 -Validate` runs that
  subcommand afterwards, and it names two suppressions, because two ICEs
  describe a package this one cannot be:

    * ICE38 wants every component installed under the user profile keyed on an
      HKCU registry value rather than on a file, so that repairing one user's
      install cannot lay files into another user's profile. Every component
      authored by hand below now is — the same rule StartMenuShortcut already
      followed, applied to the six file components too. The components the
      `Files` element generates for the Python trees cannot be: WiX gives each
      generated component a File key path and offers no way to supply a
      registry one. Suppressing ICE38 covers only those, and a perUser package
      has exactly one profile for a repair to reach into.

    * ICE64 wants every directory created in the user profile listed in the
      RemoveFile table. The four this file creates are listed, in
      InstallFolderRemoval below. The subdirectories the harvested trees bring
      with them are not, because their names are not known here. The concern
      ICE64 encodes is a per-machine install seeding directories into profiles
      it will never clean up; this package installs into one profile and
      removes what it put there.

  The alternative WiX recommends — Scope="perUserOrMachine" under
  ProgramFiles6432Folder — is ICE-clean, and it is deliberately not taken. It
  makes the default install a machine-wide one behind a UAC prompt, and it
  moves the App Paths key and the PATH entry to HKLM for a program whose
  hotkey, Start Menu view and plugins all belong to one user. Buying silence
  from a lint that does not describe this package with an elevation prompt the
  user gains nothing from is the wrong trade.

  WHY THE PAYLOAD DIRECTORIES AND SUPERVISED HOSTS ARE NOT OPTIONAL
  ================================================================
  `sdk_root()` and `shim_root()` look for the Python trees beside the running
  executable. The WASM and C-ABI providers likewise look for
  `crikey-wasm-host.exe` and `crikey-cabi-host.exe` beside the launcher and
  never search PATH. An install that omits any of these leaves a runtime
  unavailable only when that plugin type is invoked, so all four executables
  and both payload trees are in the always-installed feature.

  WHAT UNINSTALL REMOVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
  ============================================
  Removes: everything under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\CriKey that this package
  installed and the directories it created to hold it, the Start Menu folder
  and shortcut, the App Paths registration, and the per-user PATH entry
  (Part="last", Permanent="no", so the value is removed from PATH and the rest
  of PATH is left as found).

  Deliberately keeps: %APPDATA%\CriKey (configuration, plugin state, the
  startup journal) and %LOCALAPPDATA%\CriKey (icon and catalog caches). No
  RemoveFile, RemoveFolder or RemoveRegistryKey element in this file names
  either path, and none names %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs either, which every
  other per-user install on the machine shares. Uninstalling a launcher and
  reinstalling it must not silently discard the user's configured plugins and
  hotkeys; a user who wants that gone can delete two directories, and a user
  who did not want it gone cannot get it back.

  BUILD
  =====
    wix build packaging\windows\crikey.wxs -d Version=0.1.0 -d StageDir=<stage>
  StageDir must contain crikey.exe, crikey-launcher.exe, crikey-wasm-host.exe,
  crikey-cabi-host.exe, LICENSE.txt, NOTICE.md, modern-sdk\ and legacy-shim\,
  and python-runtime\ when -d PythonRuntime=1 is also passed.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">
  <Package
      Name="CriKey"
      Manufacturer="The CriKey Authors"
      Version="$(var.Version)"
      UpgradeCode="D5DB5199-A3AB-4B97-80BC-199D68D4839E"
      Scope="perUser"
      Compressed="yes"
      Codepage="65001">

    <!--
      An upgrade replaces the previous version in place. Scheduled after
      InstallValidate so the old files are removed before the new ones are laid
      down, which avoids the case where a file that was dropped between
      versions survives into the new install.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade
        Schedule="afterInstallValidate"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of CriKey is already installed. Uninstall it first if you really want this older one." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!--
      Add/Remove Programs metadata. No ARPPRODUCTICON: no icon is checked into
      this repository, and pointing the entry at an icon that is not there
      produces a broken entry rather than the default one.
    -->
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/crikey-launcher/crikey" />
    <Property Id="ARPNOREPAIR" Value="1" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="LocalAppDataFolder">
      <!--
        %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs is the convention Windows itself uses for
        per-user application installs. Note that it is a sibling of, not the
        same as, %LOCALAPPDATA%\CriKey, which holds the cache the uninstaller
        leaves behind.
      -->
      <Directory Id="ProgramsFolder" Name="Programs">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="CriKey">
          <Directory Id="ModernSdkFolder" Name="modern-sdk" />
          <Directory Id="LegacyShimFolder" Name="legacy-shim" />
<?ifdef PythonRuntime ?>
          <Directory Id="PythonRuntimeFolder" Name="python-runtime" />
<?endif?>
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="CriKey" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="CriKey" Level="1" AllowAbsent="no">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="Executable" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="Legal" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ModernSdk" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="LegacyShim" />
<?ifdef PythonRuntime ?>
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="PythonRuntime" />
<?endif?>
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="Registration" />
    </Feature>

    <!--
      Four executables, in one directory. `crikey.exe` is the console
      command line; `crikey-launcher.exe` is the graphical entry point; the
      other two are supervised runtime workers selected by the WASM and C-ABI
      providers. All providers resolve their worker beside the running
      executable and never search PATH.

      Each component is keyed on an HKCU value rather than on its file. That
      is the per-user rule the header records for ICE38, and it is the same
      one StartMenuShortcut follows: a component installed into the user's
      profile and keyed on a file there is reinstalled by repair for every
      other user of the machine. A registry key path also means an explicit
      component GUID, because WiX only generates one for a file key path.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="Executable" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="CrikeyExecutable" Guid="C059B4CD-53BF-4EF9-A016-7DD26C5C0810">
        <File Id="CrikeyExe" Source="$(var.StageDir)\crikey.exe" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="CrikeyExecutable"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="CrikeyLauncherExecutable" Guid="F842C361-F5E7-4B63-80FD-94ED55428A1F">
        <File Id="CrikeyLauncherExe" Source="$(var.StageDir)\crikey-launcher.exe" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="CrikeyLauncherExecutable"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="CrikeyWasmHostExecutable" Guid="C1D48A63-EEF6-4485-9246-3080E72A2C2E">
        <File Id="CrikeyWasmHostExe" Source="$(var.StageDir)\crikey-wasm-host.exe" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="CrikeyWasmHostExecutable"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="CrikeyCAbiHostExecutable" Guid="727C10DC-A529-43F1-A22E-579DF6BC3796">
        <File Id="CrikeyCAbiHostExe" Source="$(var.StageDir)\crikey-cabi-host.exe" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="CrikeyCAbiHostExecutable"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!--
      Spec 14.13: the licence and the attribution notice ship with the artefact.
      LICENSE has no extension in the repository and is renamed on the way in,
      because a Windows user who double-clicks an extensionless file gets the
      "how do you want to open this" dialog instead of the licence.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="Legal" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="LicenseFile" Guid="7E46121A-E02B-4B28-9DF8-DEDB6E38F02E">
        <File Id="LicenseTxt" Source="$(var.StageDir)\LICENSE.txt" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="LicenseFile"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="NoticeFile" Guid="0AEBB15A-11AD-4A43-A8C1-5BE2A32BF1C6">
        <File Id="NoticeMd" Source="$(var.StageDir)\NOTICE.md" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="NoticeFile"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!--
      Harvested rather than listed. These two trees are the Python worker
      entry points and the compatibility shim; their file lists change whenever
      the shim grows a module, and a hand-maintained list that falls one file
      behind produces an install that fails only when a plugin happens to
      import the missing module.

      The base directory of each Include is the path before the first wildcard,
      so relative subdirectories are preserved under the target folder.
      build.ps1 stages these directories with __pycache__ already removed:
      bytecode compiled by whatever interpreter last ran in the source tree is
      stale on a user's machine.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="ModernSdk" Directory="ModernSdkFolder">
      <Files Include="$(var.StageDir)\modern-sdk\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="LegacyShim" Directory="LegacyShimFolder">
      <Files Include="$(var.StageDir)\legacy-shim\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

<?ifdef PythonRuntime ?>
    <!--
      Only present when build.ps1 was given a python-build-standalone archive.
      Without it CriKey falls back to an interpreter already on the machine;
      the installer does not claim to provide one it did not stage.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="PythonRuntime" Directory="PythonRuntimeFolder">
      <Files Include="$(var.StageDir)\python-runtime\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>
<?endif?>

    <ComponentGroup Id="Registration">
      <!--
        The shortcut targets `crikey-launcher.exe` and passes no arguments.
        `crikey.exe` is the console CLI — bare, it prints usage and exits, and
        being a console-subsystem binary it would also flash a console window
        beside the launcher. `crikey-launcher.exe` is the GUI-subsystem entry
        point from the same crate (crates/crikey-cli/src/bin/crikey-launcher.rs)
        and does neither.
      -->
      <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Directory="ShortcutFolder" Guid="2C96F878-CBB7-49CF-AF9E-47A02704ACFD">
        <Shortcut Id="CrikeyShortcut"
                  Name="CriKey"
                  Description="Keyboard launcher"
                  Target="[#CrikeyLauncherExe]"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" Directory="ShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
        <!--
          A shortcut cannot be a component key path, so the component needs
          one of its own. HKCU is also what makes this a valid per-user
          component: a per-user component keyed on a file under the user's
          profile is reinstalled by repair for every other user of the
          machine.
        -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="StartMenuShortcut"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!--
        `App Paths` is how `Win+R` then `crikey` and ShellExecute find the
        executable without touching PATH; the PATH entry is for a terminal.
        Both are per-user (HKCU, System="no") to match the install scope, and
        both are removed on uninstall: the registry key because MSI removes
        registry values it created, the PATH fragment because Permanent="no"
        and Part="last" mean the Environment table subtracts exactly what it
        added.
      -->
      <Component Id="AppPathsRegistration" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="2D043DA9-E0BA-4AE2-8B72-D0C5245213D8">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\App Paths\crikey.exe"
                       Value="[#CrikeyExe]"
                       Type="string"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\App Paths\crikey.exe"
                       Name="Path"
                       Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]"
                       Type="string" />
      </Component>

      <Component Id="PathEntry" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="25AE5A80-A528-408F-8B86-D3D0B94CBA6F">
        <Environment Id="UserPath"
                     Name="PATH"
                     Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]"
                     Action="set"
                     Part="last"
                     System="no"
                     Permanent="no" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="PathEntry"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!--
        The directories this package creates in the user's profile, listed so
        that an uninstall does not leave an empty tree behind: that listing is
        what ICE64 asks for, and Windows Installer removes each of these only
        once it is already empty. ProgramsFolder is deliberately absent —
        %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs is shared with every other per-user install on
        the machine — and so are %APPDATA%\CriKey and %LOCALAPPDATA%\CriKey,
        which this package never created and must not delete.
      -->
      <Component Id="InstallFolderRemoval" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="ABD5C77B-ADDE-4D70-84CB-BF076538415C">
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveModernSdkFolder" Directory="ModernSdkFolder" On="uninstall" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveLegacyShimFolder" Directory="LegacyShimFolder" On="uninstall" />
<?ifdef PythonRuntime ?>
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemovePythonRuntimeFolder" Directory="PythonRuntimeFolder" On="uninstall" />
<?endif?>
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveInstallFolder" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" On="uninstall" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\CriKey\Installed"
                       Name="InstallFolderRemoval"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Package>
</Wix>
